    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable",
    "problem": "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$, has a cusp at $x=1,$ and is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=-2$\n- It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$",
    "worked_solution": "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$",
